{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And you shall make the altar of acacia wood.</b> In Tanchuma (10) it concludes: they committed שְׁטוּת and provoked Me with the calf — let acacia wood (עֲצֵי שִׁטִּים) come and atone for their שְׁטוּת. For every sinner is entered by a spirit of שְׁטוּת (Sotah 3a); for this reason Solomon designates the wicked as כְּסִילִים. And therefore it is said of the altar, ״נְבוּב לוּחֹת תַּעֲשֶׂה אֹתוֹ״ (Exodus 27:8), for anyone who is without knowledge and understanding is called אִישׁ נָבוּב, as it says (Job 11:12) ״וְאִישׁ נָבוּב יִלָּבֵב.״ Rashi explains there that anyone who is hollow and empty (חָלוּל וְנָבוּב) without knowledge and understanding must take for himself a heart to return in repentance. ״וְשָׁלֹשׁ אַמּוֹת קוֹמָתוֹ״ (Exodus 27:1) — the height of an average man. ״וְחָמֵשׁ אַמּוֹת אֹרֶךְ וְחָמֵשׁ אַמּוֹת רֹחַב״ (Exodus 27:1) — that makes ten, corresponding to the ten things that come from father and mother: flesh, blood, sinews, etc., enumerated in tractate Niddah (31a). And through them every sin and every transgression occurs, and it is the half-shekel of ten gerah. ״וְעָשִׂיתָ קַרְנֹתָיו״ (Exodus 27:2) — to atone for the sinner, who is likened to a ram with horns, butting upward, as it says (Psalms 75:5–6) ״וְלָרְשָׁעִים אַל תָּרִימוּ קָרֶן, אַל תָּרִימוּ לַמָּרוֹם קַרְנְכֶם,״ as explained above in parashat Vayera at the verse ״וְהִנֵּה אַיִל אַחַר נֶאֱחַז״ (Genesis 22:13). ״וְצִפִּיתָ אֹתוֹ נְחֹשֶׁת״ (Exodus 27:2) — Rashi explains: to atone for brazenness of forehead, as it says (Isaiah 48:4) ״וּמִצְחֲךָ נְחוּשָׁה.״ And this fits our words, for every קֶרֶן is in the forehead."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:23:39.369753690Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}